Slide 4 : The "Shared Drive" Problem
"Let's just use Google Drive / SharePoint / Dropbox!"
The Problem: "File Locking" and...
Silent Overwrites: Jane and John open the same file. Jane saves. John saves 5 minutes later. Jane's work is GONE.
This is a "last-save-wins" race.
Slide 6: The Solution: Version Control System (VCS)
Analogy: It's "Track Changes" for your entire project.
Analogy 2: It's a "Time Machine" for your files.
It's a system that records every change, who made it, and why... forever.
Slide 7: The Core Benefits (The "WIIFM?")
Accountability: An unchangeable log of who changed what and when. (Your Audit Trail)
Collaboration: A clear, safe process for merging work from 10s or 1000s of people.
Risk Management: You can never lose work. You can "undo" any change, even from 6 months ago.
Efficiency: Allows for "parallel work." (More on this later).
Slide 8: It's NOT Just For Code!
This is the key takeaway. You can (and should) version:
Financial Models (.xlsx)
Business Plans & Reports (.docx)
Marketing Copy (.txt, .md)
Legal Documents (Contracts, Terms of Service)
Presentations (like this one!) (.pptx)
Website Content & Designs
Slide 9: Git vs. GitHub (The Tool vs. The Hub)
Visual:
Git: The "engine" on your computer. It's the software that tracks changes. (Think: Microsoft Word).
GitHub: The "cloud website" where your team shares the project. It's the collaboration hub. (Think: Google Docs / SharePoint).
(Alternatives: GitLab, Bitbucket)
Slide 10: The Big Idea: "Commit" (A "Save Point")
In Git, you don't just "save." You "commit."
A Commit is a snapshot of your entire project at a specific moment.
It's a "save point" with a message attached.
Slide 11: The Art of the Commit Message (A Manager's View)
A commit message answers the "Why?"
Bad Message: "fixes", "stuff", "wip"
Good Message (for a manager):
"Update Q4 forecast with new sales data"
"Approve final marketing copy for launch"
"Correct calculation error in budget model"
This is your project's live audit log.
Slide 12: The "Three Areas" (Simplified Analogy)
Visual:
1. Working Folder: Your messy desk.
2. Staging Area (The "Box"): The changes you've selected to include in the next "save point."
3. Repository (The "Filing Cabinet"): The permanent, safe history of all your "commits."
Why? It forces you to be intentional about what you save.

