Part 3: Branching: The "What If?" Feature (10 minutes)
Slides 23-33: The Magic of Parallel Work
Slide 23: The "One Version" Problem
What if you want to...
...draft a new proposal, but the current one is still in use?
...let a junior employee try to edit the budget (without risk)?
...have Legal and Marketing edit the same document at the same time?
Slide 24: The Solution: Branching!
A Branch is a "parallel version" or "draft space."
The main branch is your "Official" / "Published" version.
You create a new branch (e.g., "Draft-Q4-Report") to do your work safely.
Slide 25: Branching (Visual Analogy)
Visual:
main continues untouched.
Your new branch feat-new-idea "branches off" so you can work in isolation.
Slide 26: The Business Case for Branching
Risk Management: You cannot break the "live" version.
Parallel Work: Legal can work on update-terms while Marketing works on new-homepage. They don't block each other.
Experimentation: Create a branch to test a new financial model. If it's a bad idea? Just delete the branch. No harm done.
Slide 27: Branching in GitHub Desktop
Visual:
It's just a button: "New Branch".
You give it a name: legal-review-of-terms.
You are now in a "parallel universe."
Slide 28: Branch Naming (A Management Policy)
Good branch names are like good meeting agendas.
Bad: johns-branch, test
Good: feat/user-login, fix/typo-on-homepage, draft/q4-legal-review
Slide 29: Merging: Bringing It All Back
What it is: Bringing the changes from your "draft" branch back into the "official" main branch.
Analogy: Accepting "Track Changes" from a colleague.
Slide 30: Merging (Visual)
Visual:
You take the "commit" from your draft branch...
...and add it to the main branch.
main is now up-to-date with your new work.
Slide 31: The "Scary" Part: Merge Conflicts
What it is: You and a colleague changed the exact same line on different branches.
Business Analogy: You and Jane both edited Line 5 of the contract, but with different text.
Slide 32: Conflicts are a Feature, Not a Bug!
Git stops and says: "I cannot make this business decision. A human must choose."
Visual:
The Solution: You (the manager) look at the two versions and decide which one is correct (or combine them).
This prevents "silent overwrites." It is your safety net.
Slide 33: Branching Workflow Summary
Create a new branch (a "draft").
Do your work (commit, commit, commit).
When ready, Merge your branch back into main.
(Optional) Delete the draft branch.
