Slides 34-45: The "Hub" for Managers
Slide 34: Sharing Your Work
So far, all "commits" are just on your laptop.
push: "Syncs" your local commits up to the shared GitHub.com website.
pull: "Syncs" changes from your teammates down to your laptop.
Visual:
Slide 35: The Problem: How to Merge Safely?
You finished your "Draft-Q4-Report" branch.
You could just merge it into main and push...
...but what if it's wrong? What if it needs approval?
Slide 36: The Solution: The Pull Request (PR)
This is the single most important concept for a manager.
A PR is a formal request to merge your branch.
It's a "Request for Review."
It's a "Request for Approval."
Slide 37: The Pull Request Lifecycle (The Business Workflow)
Visual:
1. Propose: You push your branch and "Open a Pull Request" on GitHub.
2. Review: You assign your manager. "Hi @Jane_Manager, please review this new Q4 forecast."
3. Discuss: Jane leaves comments on the files ("This number seems low," "Please check this calculation").
4. Iterate: You make changes on your laptop, commit, and push again. The PR updates automatically.
5. Approve: Jane clicks "Approve."
6. Merge: Only then is the work merged into the "official" main branch.
Slide 38: The Value of Pull Requests (For Managers)
Quality Control: Nothing gets into the "official" version without review.
Transparency: Everyone can see what is being proposed and why.
Knowledge Sharing: Junior team members learn from senior review.
Audit Trail: You have a permanent, timestamped record of every approval.
Slide 39: Protecting Your "Official" Branch
On GitHub, you can protect your main branch.
Visual:
Rule: "Cannot merge without 1 approval."
Rule: "Cannot merge if automated tests fail."
This is your ultimate managerial control.
Slide 40: GitHub is Also a Project Management Tool
It's not just for files. It's for managing the work.
Issues: Task tracking, bug reports, and discussion items.
(e.g., "Task: Draft Q4 Marketing Copy," "Bug: Typo on homepage")
Slide 41: GitHub Issues
Visual:
Like a Trello card or Asana task.
You can assign people, add labels (bug, marketing, urgent), and have discussions.
Slide 42: GitHub Projects (Kanban)
Visual:
You can visualize all your "Issues" on a Kanban board.
Columns: "To Do," "In Progress," "In Review," "Done."
You can drag-and-drop tasks.
Slide 43: Connecting It All
You can link your "commits" to your "issues."
Commit Message: "Fixes #42: Correct typo on homepage."
When this commit is merged... GitHub automatically closes the task.
The work and the record of the work are linked.
Slide 44: What is a "Fork"? (Open Source Model)
Analogy: You want to propose a change to a project from a different company.
Fork: You make your own copy of their entire project on GitHub.
You make changes to your copy.
You send a Pull Request from your fork back to the original.
This is how global "Open Source" collaboration works.
Slide 45: Collaboration Summary
push / pull to sync with the team.
Pull Requests (PRs) are the process for quality, review, and approval.
Issues & Projects let you manage the tasks around the work.

